---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ReviewLoop from '../components/ReviewLoop.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';

const toc = [
  { id: 'task', label: 'The task' },
  { id: 'collections', label: 'Frozen collections' },
  { id: 'loop', label: 'The review loop' },
  { id: 'submissions', label: 'What participants submit' },
  { id: 'scoring', label: 'Scoring' },
  { id: 'conditions', label: 'Workflow conditions' },
  { id: 'leakage', label: 'Leakage safeguards' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Data and evaluation"
  description="How the workshop's review/update tasks are built, what systems submit, and how submissions are scored against human and computational cost."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Data and evaluation</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Each task pairs a published systematic review with its later update. An agent works from the review's
    protocol against a frozen snapshot of the literature, and the published update, adjudicated by
    methodologists, resolves the task.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="task">
    <h2 id="task">The task</h2>
    <p>A system receives three inputs:</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div>
        <dt>Protocol</dt>
        <dd>The review question, the eligibility criteria and the outcomes of interest.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Frozen collection</dt>
        <dd>The literature as it stood on the review's search date (see below).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Resource budget</dt>
        <dd>A limit on human judgments and on compute.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Within that budget the system chooses its own searches across sources, screens titles, abstracts and
      full texts, extracts outcome data, assesses risk of bias, runs the meta-analysis in code and writes the
      synthesis. Nothing is pre-filtered: there is no Boolean candidate set to screen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="collections">
    <h2 id="collections">Frozen collections and review/update pairs</h2>
    <p>
      A published review and its later update form a natural experiment. For each pair we freeze the
      literature (bibliographic databases, trial registries and preprints) twice: once at the original
      review's search date and once at the update's search date.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the first stage the agent produces the review from its protocol against the first snapshot. In the
      second stage it updates its own review against the second snapshot. The published update resolves the
      task, and methodologists adjudicate its conclusions: did the pooled effect change direction, did it
      cross a decision threshold, did the certainty of evidence move?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="loop">
    <h2 id="loop">The review loop</h2>
    <p>
      Inside each stage an agent cycles through the same steps until it decides to stop. The decision to
      search again is the step the workshop evaluates most closely: searching for confirmation and stopping too
      early are the two failure modes that matter most.
    </p>
    <ReviewLoop />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="submissions">
    <h2 id="submissions">What participants submit</h2>
    <p>Every run submits four things:</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div>
        <dt>Selected studies</dt>
        <dd>The studies the system included, each linked to its reports.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Eligibility decisions</dt>
        <dd>Include and exclude decisions with the evidence that supports each one.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Synthesis</dt>
        <dd>The review's findings with citations and explicit uncertainty.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Action log</dt>
        <dd>Every search, every judgment, every human intervention and the stopping decision, in an open log format.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="scoring">
    <h2 id="scoring">How submissions are scored</h2>
    <p>Scoring connects three qualities of a finished review to the effort it took to produce:</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div>
        <dt>Evidence coverage</dt>
        <dd>Did the system find the studies that matter for the conclusion?</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Decision correctness</dt>
        <dd>Were eligibility, extraction and appraisal decisions right, and supported by evidence?</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Synthesis and conclusion quality</dt>
        <dd>Does the conclusion agree with the adjudicated update, and is its uncertainty stated honestly?</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Each is reported against the human and computational cost the run consumed. The cost of a missed study
      depends on what else was found, as <a href={`${href('/')}#figure-1`}>Figure 1 on the call page</a> shows, so we report
      how system rankings change with the assumed price of a missed study rather than naming a single winner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="conditions">
    <h2 id="conditions">Workflow conditions</h2>
    <p>Systems are compared under four ways of spending human effort:</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div>
        <dt>Screen until stopping</dt>
        <dd>The system decides when it has seen enough.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Fixed human budget</dt>
        <dd>A set number of human judgments, used wherever the system chooses.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Budget, then machine completion</dt>
        <dd>Humans screen up to the budget; the machine completes the rest.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model-routed selective review</dt>
        <dd>The model decides which items a human should review.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="leakage">
    <h2 id="leakage">Leakage safeguards</h2>
    <p>
      Models have read the published reviews. We evaluate on reviews and updates published after model training
      cutoffs. For each task we also record a zero-shot prediction of the conclusion, made without retrieval,
      and score only the improvement over it.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .defs {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .defs > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0 var(--space-5);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .defs > div {
      grid-template-columns: 11rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .defs dt {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    font-weight: 600;
    padding-top: 0.2rem;
  }

  .defs dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  h1 + .lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
